# Delivery Verification

Before marking a coding task complete or handing changes to the user, run verification and fix failures first.

## Required commands

From the repo root:

```bash
bun run check
bun run typecheck
```

When changes are scoped to a single package, you may run filtered checks instead:

```bash
bun run --filter @sidui/api check
bun run --filter @sidui/api typecheck
```

Replace `@sidui/api` with the package you touched (`@sidui/ui`, `@sidui/db`, etc.).

## When to run

- After implementing or modifying code
- Before saying work is done, tests pass, or a PR/commit is ready
- **Before every git commit** (see Commit workflow below)
- After fixing lint, format, or type errors reported by the user or CI

## Commit workflow

When the user asks to commit:

1. Run `bun run check` and `bun run typecheck` (or package-scoped equivalents).
2. If check fails, run `bun run fix` in the affected package(s), then re-run `check`.
3. If errors remain after fix, **stop — do not commit**. Report the failures and fix them first.
4. Only when both commands pass, stage and commit (including any auto-formatted changes from fix).

## On failure

- Fix Biome/format issues (`bun run fix` in the affected package, then re-run `check`)
- Fix TypeScript errors before delivery
- Do not claim completion without command output showing success
- Do not commit while check or typecheck is failing

## Optional (when relevant)

- `bun run test` — when behavior changed or tests were added/updated
- `bun run --filter <package> test` — for package-scoped test runs
